{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 260,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s magnetic field protects us from\ndeadly space radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4440,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, what if it were drastically weakened? For example, as a precursor to it flipping upside down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11240,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, it has before … many, many times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20100,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spaceship Earth has a literal deflector shield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22890,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A geomagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24570,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lines of magnetic force, forged by currents\nin the planet’s molten core, erupt from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29970,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surface close to the north south geographic\npoles, connecting to each other to wreath"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet in a dipole field, like a gigantic\nbar magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38840,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic fields exert a force on moving charged\nparticles, causing them to spiral around those force lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46409,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, that’s helpful, because Earth is constantly\nbombarded by very fast moving charged particles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52379,
    "dDurationMs": 2631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially coming from the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55010,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our magnetic field deflects the worst of these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57900,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not all planets are so lucky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60370,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars, with its solid core, has no such shield\n– and so the red planet’s atmosphere was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stripped away by the solar wind billions of\nyears ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70160,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what would happen if Earth lost its field?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73260,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Would we lose our atmosphere?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75420,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Would life be extincted by crazy space radiation?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79070,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, may get to find out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81600,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic field is currently undergoing\nrapid changes, possibly signaling the imminent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87070,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flipping of its polarity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89200,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The north pole may become the south, and the\nsouth the north."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92810,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is called a geomagnetic reversal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96060,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And during that reversal we’d be left relatively\nunprotected for thousands of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101630,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll come back to the current situation\nand whether we should be concerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106270,
    "dDurationMs": 3389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, why do we think such a thing could\nhappen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109659,
    "dDurationMs": 2961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, because it has in the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112620,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113920,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see it in the geological record."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116790,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic materials like iron often form with\ntheir natural fields aligned with Earth’s field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122580,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can track the direction of Earth’s magnetic\nfield in sedimentary layers and in old volcanic flows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129720,
    "dDurationMs": 7820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Turns out Earth’s field has completely flipped\ndirection 183 times over the past 84 million years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137540,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– so a little more than once per half\na million years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141480,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last full geomagnetic reversal was over\n700,000 years ago – so you might say we’re past due."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149600,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, not exactly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152370,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact these flips seem to be pretty random\nevents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156860,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We may be no more “due” than we were at\nany other time in the past half million years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162820,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except for the fact that the magnetic field\nDOES seem to be acting strangely lately."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to really understand whether a flip is\nlikely, we need to try to understand how they happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And for that we need to understand the Earth’s\nmagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178360,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally we think of magnetic fields being\ngenerated in two way: In magnetic materials"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184319,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like iron, the sum total of the tiny magnetic\nfields of their constituent particles align"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190519,
    "dDurationMs": 2071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to give a global field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192590,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s your bar magnet or fridge magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195840,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alternatively, flows of many charged particles\nlike electrons – so electrical currents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201869,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- can produce magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203989,
    "dDurationMs": 2451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example in an electromagnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Earth’s interior is not intrinsically\nmagnetic – its too hot for the iron atoms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212200,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the core to spontaneously align."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214420,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And although the interior is rotating, it’s\nelectrically neutral – so there shouldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218750,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be an overall electrical current."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221580,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How, then, does the Earth generate such a\ngigantic and well-organized dipole magnetic field?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228840,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with a quick review of what the\ninterior of the Earth look like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233959,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beneath the thin crust and 2900 km of solid\nmantle lies earth’s core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240809,
    "dDurationMs": 5661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 2400 km thick outer core is molten iron\nand nickel, and some other stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246470,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not talking about “lava” here – I’m\ntalking about liquid metal with the viscosity of water."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252600,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beneath this layer is the inner core - a 1200\nkm radius ball of solid iron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258460,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s solid because of the pressure at that\ndepth – at around 5500 Kelvin temperature,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263660,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would instantly melt at lower pressures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267020,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everything is moving down there – the solid\ncore rotates slightly more quickly than the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273280,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The core's day is a few seconds shorter\nthan a surface day. The outer core has a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279160,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation gradient – the outer layer actually\nrotates a bit slower than the surface,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284300,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it speeds up as you get deeper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286630,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that motion gets messier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288650,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of the Earth is cooling down\nvery slowly, which means the liquid outer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292910,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core is freezing into the solid inner core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295600,
    "dDurationMs": 7420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the inner core grows it releases non-iron\nimpurities that flow upwards, joining convection streams."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303020,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These flows are then twisted into helixes\nby the coriolis force – the same effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308200,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that produces hurricanes on Earth’s surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310980,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s all of this motion that together produces\nEarth’s magnetic field through a process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315780,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the dynamo effect – or so most scientists\naccept these days."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321080,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And dynamo theory not only explains geomagnetism,\nbut also why Earth’s field sometimes reverses its polarity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329800,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s how it works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332140,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that motion I just described, electrons\nand nuclei should all be moving together –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337420,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so no electrical current."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339460,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So where does the magnetism come from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342030,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key is that the dynamo effect doesn’t\nreally create a magnetic field from scratch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347090,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– instead it amplifies, organizes, and sustains\nan existing field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352830,
    "dDurationMs": 2910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll come back to where that initial magnetic\nfield comes from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355740,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, let’s say that we start with some\nweak dipole field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360620,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That field passes through the liquid outer\ncore, which is an electrical conductor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conductors have this cool property that they\ndrag magnetic fields with them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370440,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the entire core is rotating with the\nEarth then the magnetic field will also rotate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376710,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But remember that the rotation of the outer\ncore gets faster towards the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result, the starting magnetic field gets\nwound up into rings around the axis of rotation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387240,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– into a torus shape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390450,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now cut to the second type of motion in the\nouter core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393730,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have these streams of conducting material\ntwisted into helices by the coriolis force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400200,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those flows grab hold of our toroidal magnetic\nfield and twist it up further - into many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406010,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little loops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407460,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those loops form magnetic tubes around Earth’s\nrotational axis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412130,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These in turn generate toroidal electrical\ncurrents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416240,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we have exactly the conditions of an electromagnet\n– organized rings of current, which produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422520,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our giant dipole field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425330,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so start with a weak dipole field and\nyou get a strong one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429780,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where does that initial magnetic field\ncome from in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, actually ANY weak field – even random\nbits of field – for example thermal fluctuations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440080,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- are enough to initiate this runaway effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443450,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once started, the field builds to maximum\nstrength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447410,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact any rotating body with a fluid conductor\ncan produce such a field – the Earth, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453681,
    "dDurationMs": 8459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also the Sun with its flowing hydrogen plasma,\nor the liquid metallic hydrogen in Jupiter and Saturn’s cores."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462140,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The field produced by this effect looks pretty\norganized, but it’s not as clean as a bar magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468180,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It shifts and moves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470520,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact Earth’s magnetic field is a highly\ndynamic beast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474620,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The north and south gomagnetic poles are close\nto the geographic poles – so, close to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479960,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s rotation axis, but are not quite exactly aligned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484060,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact they move and shift."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486160,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic north pole is currently moving\nat around 60 km per year around 5 degrees"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492220,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "south of the geographic pole, leaving Canadian\nterritory and heading to Siberia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498389,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strength of the field across the surface\nalso changes, and all of these shifts are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503480,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to changing flows within the outer core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507340,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what’s all this about the magnetic\nfield flipping over?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510830,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, HOW can it flip? – surely the direction\nof the magnetic field depends on the direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515830,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth is spinning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517700,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually no – it depends on the direction\nof these giant electrical currents,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523220,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which in turn depend on the direction of small magnetic\nloops generated by these helical convection flows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530740,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we expect that if the magnetic field\nwere switched off entirely, it would reestablish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535680,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "itself randomly, with the north and south\nmagnetic poles aligned either one way or the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543000,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the geological record there seems to be\nno pattern to when the field flips, nor to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548220,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which alignment is preferred."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550520,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s probably how the flip happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552860,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s magnetic field isn’t necessarily\nswitched off, but it’s scrambled in some way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558300,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It then builds up again, choosing its direction\nrandomly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561880,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When it does a full flip we call it a geomagnetic\nreversal, and when the field just glitches but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567580,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ends up in the same direction it started we call it a\ngeomagnetic excursion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573400,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few ideas on how these glitches\nmight happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be that some event triggers a disruption\nin the flows within the outer core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581640,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could be an asteroid or comet impact,\nan interaction between the core and mantle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586250,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– for example the formation of a new magma\nplume or the subduction of a continental plate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591810,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no clear evidence of any of these\ntriggers, however - and so most scientists think these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597750,
    "dDurationMs": 7820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geomagnetic events are just a natural part of dynamo\nbehavior, in which the chaotic motion of outer-core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605570,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluid causes a tangling of magnetic field\nlines and a global drop in field strength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611529,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the question you should be asking is\nhow do we know all of this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615860,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the fact is, it’s not easy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618360,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Computer simulations show that the dynamo\neffect should indeed produce a large-scale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623500,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dipole field that spontaneously reverses,\nalthough the details are still a little elusive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629980,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those who don’t trust computer simulations\n– how about building our own giant spinning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634860,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ball of molten metal?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636660,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are several of these liquid sodium experiments\nin operation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640700,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They simulate the dynamo effect in the outer\ncore quite nicely, and even reveal spontaneous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646140,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "polarity flips."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647900,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever the mechanism for geomagnetic reversals\nreally is – we know it happens, and will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653140,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surely happen again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654860,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it happening now?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657340,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The international World Magnetic Model is\na global maps of Earth’s magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662241,
    "dDurationMs": 6659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "updated every 5 years – in the past that’s\nbeen frequent enough to account for small changes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668900,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not any more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670940,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The WMM scientists found the north magnetic\npole was moving so quickly that they updated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677940,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nearly a year early – at the beginning of\n2019, and they’ll update again at the end of the year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684140,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, does this mean the field is preparing\nto flip?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687340,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mmm... not really."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689020,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, maybe - but we know that the field must\nfluctuate quite a bit even when it’s not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694730,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about to reverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696170,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll need to see a lot more disruption\nbefore we start to worry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699800,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How bad is that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700900,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701660,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A bit bad, probably."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703280,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not catastrophic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704780,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no evidence of increased extinction\nrates associated with any of the past reversals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710280,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like I said, the field weakens but doesn’t\nswitch off completely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714960,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there may be higher incidents of cancer\nand other mutation from more high energy particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719880,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaching the ground, and probably we’ll\nhave to get much better at shielding satellites"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724450,
    "dDurationMs": 1630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the solar wind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726080,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The field also becomes very messy – with\nmini north and south magnetic poles popping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731380,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up across the surface of the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733220,
    "dDurationMs": 4501,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So certain species of migrating bird as well\nas old-timey sea captains are going to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737721,
    "dDurationMs": 2839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very confused for a while."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740560,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the classic song goes, Magnets, how do\nthey work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744760,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately scientists have a pretty good\nidea, and think that Earth’s magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749500,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is likely to hold out for our lifetimes – and\nthose of some generations to come."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754610,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now at least we remain protected from\nthe worst ravages of solar storms, and of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759920,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our dangerously irradiated space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763480,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, everyone -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764600,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Comment Responses will return shortly, but\nbefore we sign off today there are two important things:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, we’d like to thank all Patreon supporters. You guys really help us keep the lights on,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we'd specially like to thank Morgan Hough, for joining the ranks of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781840,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Big Bang contributors on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Morgan, we’re going to spend your contribution\non building a house made of millions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789480,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refrigerator magnets to protect us once the\nEarth’s magnetic field fails."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794180,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You will be invited."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795760,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, until then, Space Time and PBS Digital\nStudios want to hear from all of YOU."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800620,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every year we do a survey that asks about\nwhat you’re into, your favorite PBS shows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805400,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and things you’d like to see more of from\nPBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808980,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Time fans have always been amazing at\ntaking the time and making their voices heard"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813760,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the survey and we’d love for that to\ncontinue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816740,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We really do take a look at the results and\ndiscuss them because all of this data helps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821300,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us understand you better and make more of\nthe stuff YOU want to see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825720,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The survey takes about ten minutes, and you\ncould win a T-shirt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829000,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link's in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830800,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
